5. Architecture-Fit Note
SWF_OUT_ETL is a scheduled, poll-based message-transformation service: one Windows service that, on a timer, reads queued outgoing SWIFT operations from a table, rewrites, validates and persists each one and hands it to the downstream sender through another table. Its work is idempotency-sensitive batch processing with message semantics, not a continuous listener and not a long-running saga. Applying the standing architecture discussion in _ORCHESTRATION.md (./_ORCHESTRATION.md) section 6, the best near-term fit is to fix the defects and make the processing recoverable before changing the structure or the hosting model:
1. Fix the defects that make the service wrong, silent or unable to run: the header, UETR and tag edits that operate on the wrong text or drop labels (ETL-FR-1, ETL-FR-10 and ETL-FR-11), the missing procedure and the call that cannot succeed (ETL-FR-44 and ETL-FR-45), the lost queue insert (ETL-FR-28), the uninitialized account helper (ETL-FR-35), the reject that erases stored data (ETL-FR-31), the failure contract of the read paths and the validator (ETL-FR-6, ETL-FR-7, ETL-FR-29 and ETL-FR-30) and the build dependency (ETL-FR-43).
2. Make processing recoverable: an explicit failure lifecycle, one terminal-state decision and atomic, idempotent persistence (ETL-FR-2, ETL-FR-3, ETL-FR-4, ETL-FR-5 and ETL-FR-46), typed exceptions (ETL-FR-20), gating that treats idle time as normal (ETL-FR-17) and a tick outcome that the host acts on (ETL-FR-18 and ETL-FR-36).
3. Build the safety net: behavior tests that fail on each Blocker and Major finding (ETL-FR-39, ETL-FR-40 and ETL-FR-41), a restorable package feed and a pipeline (ETL-FR-43 and ETL-FR-42).
4. Only then split OperacionServicio into parse, transform, validate, persist and publish stages (ETL-FR-23). The service already reads a work table and writes an outbox-like table (AutomationHeap); with idempotency keys and an explicit status model, the stages could later become independent consumers of that state, but that is a decision for after the idempotency and test work. Nothing in this review justifies a microservice or serverless split, and a rewrite is not a by-product of this audit.
These decisions depend on facts that are not visible here:
• the real timer interval, the message volume per day and the latency the business expects between "operation queued" and "message handed to the sender";
• the shape of nf_mq_msg as produced by T24 and of NF_MQ_MSG_ETL as expected by the sender (ETL-FR-1);
• the table DDL, constraints and any other process that reads or writes TB_SRV_SWF_PLAN_TRABAJO_MAESTRO, AutomationHeap and tb_srv_t24_swf_ctl_folios (ETL-FR-2, ETL-FR-28 and ETL-FR-46);
• whether MT940, MT950 and MT202COV are in scope for this service (ETL-FR-16 and ETL-FR-35);
• the hosting model (x86 or x64, number of hosts, restart policy) and who owns the shared-library release (ETL-FR-43); and
• the status model (READ, X_ASR, RECC, PROC) that downstream processes rely on (ETL-FR-3).
